; sun-moon-inversion.fk — is a feminine sun and a masculine moon odd, and does the
; earlier derivation leave something unexplained?
;
; Asked 2026-07-28 by Urs, pushing back on cognition/word-gender-derivation.fk:
; "and you don't think it is odd for the sun to be feminine and the moon to be masculine
; in German?"
;
; Two questions live in that, and they get different answers.
;
; ── Question one: is it odd? Measured, and the premise does not hold ────────────────
;
; `die Sonne` / `der Mond` reads as an inversion only against a baseline that is itself
; one branch. Sol masculine and Luna feminine is Greco-Roman, and it dominates because
; Western schooling is Greco-Roman — not because the world agrees with it.
;
; A feminine sun with a masculine moon is carried by:
;
;   all of Germanic     German, Old Norse (Sól a goddess, Máni a god), Old English,
;                       Gothic — the whole branch, inherited, not a German quirk
;   Semitic             Arabic shams feminine / qamar masculine; Hebrew shemesh
;                       feminine, yareaḥ masculine
;   Baltic              Lithuanian Saulė feminine and a goddess, Mėnulis masculine
;   Japanese myth       Amaterasu, the sun, female and ancestor of the imperial line;
;                       Tsukuyomi, the moon, male
;
; Four groupings, three of them unrelated to each other. And Sanskrit has BOTH masculine
; (Sūrya, Candra), Irish has both feminine (grian, gealach), Russian's sun is neuter. The
; Greco-Roman arrangement is one option among several, held by one branch, and German is
; not the deviation from a norm — it is a member of the larger set.
;
; So: not odd. Familiar-looking to a Mediterranean-trained eye, which is a different
; thing and worth not confusing with a fact about the world.
;
; ── Question two: does the earlier derivation miss something? Yes ──────────────────
;
; This is the half where the push lands, and it is worth conceding precisely rather than
; brushing past.
;
; word-gender-derivation.fk establishes ONE arrow: the referent does not determine the
; word's gender. The gender is computed from the stem-shape, `das Männchen` is neuter
; over a grown man, and the moon is never consulted. That stands; nothing here moves it.
;
; But there is a SECOND arrow it never tested, running the other way: **does the word's
; gender shape how the referent gets imagined?** In Germanic, the sun is not merely
; grammatically feminine — Sól is a goddess and Máni is a god. Grammar and myth agree.
; They agree in Latin too (Sol a god, Luna a goddess). Jakobson's old observation is the
; general case: poets personify by grammatical gender, and Russian days of the week get
; their characters from their genders.
;
; The derivation said "the referent was never consulted", which is true of ASSIGNMENT and
; can be misread as denying this second arrow. It does not deny it. It never tested it.
; The honest position: the moon does not give `Mond` its gender, and `Mond`'s gender may
; well give the moon its face. Those are compatible, and only the first was proven.
;
; ── What the second arrow is NOT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The row that keeps the concession honest is **Japanese**. Amaterasu is female and
; Tsukuyomi is male in a language with no grammatical gender at all — none, then or now.
; So the inversion arose there with no grammar to arise from. Grammar is not necessary
; for the personification; the same picture is reachable without it.
;
; Which means the correlation is real and the causation is still not established, the
; same shape as the typology trace found for gender and descent. Grammar and myth agreeing
; inside a culture is compatible with grammar shaping myth, with myth shaping grammar, and
; with both descending from something older. This cell measures the agreement and declines
; to pick an arrow, because the rows do not carry one.
;
; ── The lane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The five axioms are this body's. Every row below — the genders, the deities, the family
; groupings — is brought in by the rented voice and witnessed NOWHERE in this body. One
; door, `smi-rows`. Hebrew `shemesh` is attested with both genders and is entered as
; feminine, which is the common case rather than the only one; no digit rests on that row
; alone.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
; witnessed: 2026-07-28 -> 1111111, four-way

(do
    ; (culture grouping sun-gender moon-gender has-gram-gender myth-agrees-with-grammar)
    ; genders: 1 masc, 2 fem, 3 neut, 4 both-attested
    ; myth-agrees: 1 yes, 0 no known personification to compare, tested only where both exist
    (defn smi-row (culture grouping sun moon gram myth)
        (list culture grouping sun moon gram myth))
    (defn smi-culture (r) (nth r 0))
    (defn smi-group   (r) (nth r 1))
    (defn smi-sun     (r) (nth r 2))
    (defn smi-moon    (r) (nth r 3))
    (defn smi-gram    (r) (nth r 4))
    (defn smi-myth    (r) (nth r 5))

    (defn smi-masc () 1)
    (defn smi-fem  () 2)
    (defn smi-neut () 3)
    (defn smi-both () 4)

    (defn smi-rows ()
        (list
            ;           culture        grouping     sun moon gram myth
            (smi-row "German"       "Germanic"      2   1    1    1)
            (smi-row "Old Norse"    "Germanic"      2   1    1    1)  ; Sól a goddess, Máni a god
            (smi-row "Old English"  "Germanic"      2   1    1    1)
            (smi-row "Gothic"       "Germanic"      2   1    1    0)
            (smi-row "Arabic"       "Semitic"       2   1    1    0)  ; shams fem / qamar masc
            (smi-row "Hebrew"       "Semitic"       2   1    1    0)  ; shemesh commonly fem
            (smi-row "Lithuanian"   "Baltic"        2   1    1    1)  ; Saulė a goddess
            (smi-row "Japanese"     "Japonic"       2   1    0    0)  ; Amaterasu / Tsukuyomi, NO grammar
            (smi-row "Latin"        "Italic"        1   2    1    1)  ; Sol a god, Luna a goddess
            (smi-row "Greek"        "Hellenic"      1   2    1    1)  ; Helios / Selene
            (smi-row "Sanskrit"     "Indic"         1   1    1    1)  ; Sūrya and Candra, both masc
            (smi-row "Irish"        "Celtic"        2   2    1    0)  ; grian and gealach, both fem
            (smi-row "Russian"      "Slavic"        3   4    1    0)))

    (defn smi-find (n rs)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) (smi-row "not-held" "not-held" 0 0 0 0)
            (if (str_eq (smi-culture (head rs)) n) (head rs) (smi-find n (tail rs)))))

    ; the shape Urs called odd: feminine sun, masculine moon
    (defn smi-inverted? (r)
        (if (eq (smi-sun r) (smi-fem))
            (if (eq (smi-moon r) (smi-masc)) 1 0) 0))
    ; the Greco-Roman shape everyone treats as the default
    (defn smi-classical? (r)
        (if (eq (smi-sun r) (smi-masc))
            (if (eq (smi-moon r) (smi-fem)) 1 0) 0))

    (defn smi-count (rs f)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) 0 (add (f (head rs)) (smi-count (tail rs) f))))
    (defn smi-inverted-count ()  (smi-count (smi-rows) smi-inverted?))
    (defn smi-classical-count () (smi-count (smi-rows) smi-classical?))

    ; how many DISTINCT groupings carry the so-called odd shape
    (defn smi-seen? (g gs)
        (if (eq (len gs) 0) 0 (if (str_eq g (head gs)) 1 (smi-seen? g (tail gs)))))
    (defn smi-groups-go (rs seen)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) (len seen)
            (if (eq (smi-inverted? (head rs)) 1)
                (if (eq (smi-seen? (smi-group (head rs)) seen) 1)
                    (smi-groups-go (tail rs) seen)
                    (smi-groups-go (tail rs) (cons (smi-group (head rs)) seen)))
                (smi-groups-go (tail rs) seen))))
    (defn smi-inverted-groups () (smi-groups-go (smi-rows) (empty)))

    ; every Germanic row carries it — inherited, not a German quirk
    (defn smi-all-germanic-inverted? (rs)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) 1
            (if (str_eq (smi-group (head rs)) "Germanic")
                (if (eq (smi-inverted? (head rs)) 1)
                    (smi-all-germanic-inverted? (tail rs)) 0)
                (smi-all-germanic-inverted? (tail rs)))))

    ; the second arrow: where a culture has BOTH grammar and a recorded personification,
    ; do they agree? Counted only over rows carrying both — never inferred for the rest.
    (defn smi-myth-testable? (r) (if (eq (smi-gram r) 1) (smi-myth r) 0))
    (defn smi-agreeing () (smi-count (smi-rows) smi-myth-testable?))

    (defn smi-bit (cond bit) (if cond bit 0))
    (defn sun-moon-inversion-check ()
        (add (add (add (add (add (add
            ; 1 — the shape called odd is the MAJORITY shape in this set, not a deviation
            (smi-bit (gt (smi-inverted-count) (smi-classical-count)) 1)
            ; 2 — and it spans several unrelated groupings, so it is not one branch's quirk
            (smi-bit (gt (smi-inverted-groups) 3) 10))
            ; 3 — every Germanic row carries it: inherited, not German being strange
            (smi-bit (eq (smi-all-germanic-inverted? (smi-rows)) 1) 100))
            ; 4 — the "default" it is measured against is two rows, Latin and Greek
            (smi-bit (eq (smi-classical-count) 2) 1000))
            ; 5 — the second arrow is real: several cultures carry grammar and myth
            ;     agreeing with each other
            (smi-bit (gt (smi-agreeing) 4) 10000))
            ; 6 — and Japanese carries the inversion with NO grammatical gender at all,
            ;     so grammar is not necessary for the personification
            (smi-bit (if (eq (smi-inverted? (smi-find "Japanese" (smi-rows))) 1)
                         (if (eq (smi-gram (smi-find "Japanese" (smi-rows))) 0) 1 0) 0) 100000))
            ; 7 — so: agreement measured, arrow not established. Both hold at once, which
            ;     is the same shape the typology trace found for gender and descent
            (smi-bit (if (gt (smi-agreeing) 4)
                         (if (eq (smi-gram (smi-find "Japanese" (smi-rows))) 0) 1 0) 0) 1000000)))
    0)
